<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, applied to the app and, via
  `entitlementsInherit` in electron-builder.yml, to every helper process.

  Four entries, and no more. This file replaces electron-builder's built-in
  default wholesale, so anything not listed here is not granted; the temptation
  when a signed build misbehaves is to paste in another entitlement from a blog
  post and see if the symptom goes away. Each of these has a reason written next
  to it. An entitlement that cannot be given one belongs out of this file, not in
  it with a shrug: every entry widens what a compromised renderer is allowed to
  do, and `allow-dyld-environment-variables` and `cs.debugger` in particular are
  routinely copied into Electron apps that have no use for either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code and needs pages that are both
    writable and executable to do it. The hardened runtime forbids that by
    default, and the failure is not subtle: the renderer process dies at
    startup, before anything renders.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    The wider version of the same permission, and not redundant with it.
    `allow-jit` covers memory mapped through the MAP_JIT flag; parts of V8 and
    of Chromium's own allocator map executable memory without it, and under a
    hardened runtime those allocations fail. Apple documents this pair together
    for exactly this class of app, and Electron does not work with only the
    first half.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    The one this app genuinely stands on.

    DropScribe ships four executables it did not build: `ffmpeg`, `ffprobe`,
    `whisper-cli` and `parakeet-cli`, fetched by `scripts/fetch-binaries.mjs`
    into `Resources/bin` and spawned from the main process. They come from other
    people's toolchains and carry nobody's Developer ID, least of all this
    project's, which does not have one. That is precisely the situation library
    validation exists to refuse: code living inside a hardened bundle that is not
    signed by the bundle's own team.

    Without this entitlement they do not run, and "do not run" means every
    transcription fails at the moment audio extraction starts, on a machine
    where the app launched and looked completely healthy. `enginesReady()`
    reports them present, because they are present.

    This will look like the removable line in this file to someone hardening the
    build later. It is the load-bearing one.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Outbound network: model weights are downloaded from Hugging Face on first
    use, and the cloud path uploads audio to DeepInfra, Deepgram, ElevenLabs or
    OpenRouter with the user's own key.

    Worth being straight about what this does today, because it is not what the
    other three do: this is an App Sandbox entitlement, and DropScribe is not
    sandboxed. A hardened runtime does not restrict outbound connections, so
    nothing here is currently gated by it. It is declared because it is the
    accurate statement of what the app reaches for, and because the sandbox is
    the one configuration in which its absence is enforced by turning every
    model download and every cloud job into a connection that simply never
    opens. Deleting it changes no behaviour now; adding it back later, after a
    day of debugging silent failures, is the alternative.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
